# Cloudflare Pages redirect rules.
#
# Order matters, and so does what is NOT here: static assets are matched before
# these rules, which is why the rules below do not swallow /assets/*.js or the
# prerendered dist/<route>/index.html files that US-147 writes. Rules for a
# prerendered path are rejected by the build for the same reason — see
# verifyServedLayout() in scripts/prerender.mts.
#
# WHAT CHANGED IN US-176. This file used to end with:
#
#     /* /index.html 200
#
# which answered EVERY unmatched URL with 200 and dist/index.html. Since US-147
# that file is not an empty shell, it is the fully prerendered homepage — so
# agentbio.net/anything-at-all returned the homepage's title, description and
# body under a 200, and only became the 404 page once the bundle executed. A
# client-side router cannot set a status code, so no amount of work in
# NotFound.tsx could fix it.
#
# Removing the catch-all lets Pages fall through to dist/404.html with a real
# 404. That only works if every route the SPA owns but does not prerender is
# listed below, so src/spa-routes.test.ts walks App.tsx and fails when a route
# is neither prerendered nor covered here. Adding a route to App.tsx without
# adding it to one of those two places is a broken page, and the test is what
# says so before a deploy does.

# /features has never been a route. App.tsx defines only the five /features/*
# children, so this path fell through to the SPA fallback and answered with the
# 404 page under a 200 — a soft 404 that the old hand-edited sitemap submitted
# to Google on purpose for months (US-149). The homepage's feature section is
# what the URL was reaching for.
/features / 301

# /for is the same shape: a prefix with 26 children and no index. It is a single
# segment, so the /:username rule at the bottom would answer it 200 with the
# homepage — the exact soft 404 this story removes. Sent to the homepage
# explicitly, as /features is.
#
# The other bare segments RESERVED_SEGMENTS lists but App.tsx does not route
# (/legal, /about, /contact, /help, /support, /api) are left alone: none is
# linked from anywhere, none has ever been a URL here, and each would be one
# more rule to keep true. functions/[username].ts treats them as reserved and
# they fall through to the SPA, which renders its 404 body under a 200. Worth
# knowing about; not worth six rules.
/for / 301

# --- Routes the SPA owns and the build does not prerender --------------------
#
# Each of these is behind a session, or depends on a database row, so there is
# no meaningful anonymous render to write to a file. They must answer 200 with
# the app; anything else is a working page turned into a 404.

/dashboard      /index.html 200
/dashboard/*    /index.html 200
/admin          /index.html 200
/admin/*        /index.html 200
/onboarding/*   /index.html 200

# /auth/callback and /auth/sso/callback consume an OAuth code and redirect, so
# they are not prerendered (see the note in src/config/prerender-routes.ts).
# The prerendered /auth/* pages are matched as assets before these rules are
# consulted, so this covers the callbacks without shadowing them.
/auth/*         /index.html 200

# Page-builder pages, one per tenant row.
/p/*            /index.html 200

# A public profile: /:username, and its review form. One path segment, which is
# indistinguishable from a typo without a database lookup — so this rule keeps
# every single-segment URL answering 200, and functions/[username].ts is what
# returns a real 404 to a crawler when the username is not a published profile.
#
# Deliberately NOT a catch-all: /blog/nope, /features/nope, /tools/nope,
# /vs/nope and /for/nope now reach dist/404.html and return 404, because every
# real URL under those prefixes is prerendered to a file.
/:username        /index.html 200
/:username/review /index.html 200
